WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
OpenAI launches Astra for Law, pairing GPT-6 Astra with a 230 million URL legal search index and firm specific workflow tools. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 177. OpenAI released Astra for Law today. The new offering runs on GPT-6 Astra. It adds instructions for legal analysis and writing. Settings are tuned for thorough work. A legal search index covers US case law, statutes, regulations,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.840
regulations, court rules, and administrative decisions.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.500
The index spans more than 230 million URLs

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.080
with sources added daily.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.300
Sullivan and Cromwell built an agreement analyzer

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.420
inside the platform.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.780
Ropes in gray built an M&amp;A diligence system.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.180
Cooley built Go Public for IPO preparation.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.520
Open AI is also rolling out 26 partner built plugins.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.880
There are 47 community plugins for legal work in chat GPT.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.660
Partners include Thompson Reuters, Harvey, Legora, and Imanage.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.820
Privacy and governance controls are expanding for eligible firms through trusted access.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.580
The release targets law firms and legal technology teams that need specialized context, and

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.220
thropic optimized inference for more than 30 open source biomolecular models.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.040
The optimizations deliver four times faster performance on average.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:25.920
Custom GPU software produced the gains.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:32.060
All code is on GitHub at the Uplifting-Bio-Molecular-Modeling Repository.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.560
A technical report accompanies the release.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.380
The work targets molecular structure modeling, drug-like molecule design,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.840
and genetic mutation effect prediction.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.660
The optimizations are released under an open source license

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.520
and Thropic partnered with Adaptive Bio on a protein design competition.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.340
The effort will experimentally validate over 5,000 protein designs.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.780
Up to $1 million in clawed credits are available.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.360
Funding for validation is included.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.400
Model contributes up to $250,000 in compute.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:07.980
Twist bio-science supplies DNA.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.340
Sign up details are on Adaptive's protein base.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.040
The competition demonstrates the practical impact of the inference optimizations.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:20.160
Anthropic released three measurements to track AI development.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.680
The first tracks the share of AI R&amp;D preferred by AI itself.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.960
The second measures the quality of oversight applied to AI agents.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.180
The third tracks allocation of compute resources.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.740
A snapshot from Inside and Thropic is included.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.360
Any frontier lab could publish comparable figures for third-party verification.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.220
The full post and methodology are on the Anthropic site.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.800
The measurements aim to reduce the information gap between labs and the public.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:56.300
A 421 million-parameter non-auto regressive decision model called LIA was trained on a 100%

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.800
human annotated corpus.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.000
The corpus contains over 25,000 real-world examples.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.980
Training used an RLCD policy gradient approach.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:12.000
The model pairs a bidirectional modern-burt large encoder with a scratch transformer head.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.760
It runs in a single 35 millisecond forward pass on low-end hardware.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.300
The model is available on Hugging Face at the Convi Innovation's LIA repository.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.620
A demo space and GitHub repo are included.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.480
Training ran on a single RTX60 Pro with 96GB VRAM.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:37.760
A proposed front-end back-end split lets a duplex speech-to-text front-end emit a delegation token.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.920
ASR transcripts stream to a text-based back-end LLM for tool calls.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.240
tools. Tool results return through a lightweight pre-fill and repeat mechanism.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:53.080
Streaming TTS synthesis follows. The system preserves regular duplex turn-taking

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:59.680
and interruption handling. It achieves 92-97% tool-call recall. Accuracy

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:08.380
rejecting irrelevant calls reaches 81.2%. With a larger back end such as QN3-235B-A-22B,

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:16.160
It outperforms GPT real-time Mini and Quinn-3 Omni-30B A3B Instruct on EVA Bench.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.220
The architecture requires only minimal changes to the front-end model.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.680
TrioRag retrieves independently over a shared multi-vector index.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.380
It uses the question, the anchor image, and a VLM-enhanced query.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.840
Results combine through late fusion.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.400
The approach matches or exceeds graph-based systems on three benchmarks.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:44.060
marks. Total cost drops while per-query inference accelerates by 1.6 to 2.3 times. It is paired

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:49.840
with the new AutoQA automotive benchmark built from noisy web-sourced images. The framework

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.000
avoids maintaining a corpus-level graph that would need frequent rebuilds.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.660
Cleanysurca is a multi-stage LLM framework. It produces calendar anchored, imprecision-aware

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.000
reconstructions of clinical events. The source is unstructured discharge summaries without

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.840
event-level timestamps. On 52 mimic three summaries, it extracted 15,891

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.300
temporally tagged events. After clinician correction, the timelines compress

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:22.980
source material 1.52 times into date-grouped records. Instruction tuning on

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:28.000
the resulting silver standard data improves five open-weight models. Gains appear

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.880
on event extraction, temporal tagging, and summarization. The framework was

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.140
evaluated on a clinician in the loop benchmark of verified gold standard labels.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.320
ProKDA uses an agentic background knowledge pipeline.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.840
A three-stage training sequence follows.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.220
The stages are background learning, detection learning, and boundary alignment.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:53.100
The method avoids interference between explanation, generation, and label prediction.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.400
Prior, explain-then-detect approaches were limited by that interference.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.980
It reaches state-of-the-art detection on three public hateful meme benchmarks.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:02.240
Marks.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.460
Accurate, evidence-supported explanations are produced.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.840
The staged approach was tested on three public benchmarks with consistent gains over joint

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:11.720
training baselines.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.520
Sin UC is a constraint-guided synthesis method.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.200
It represents user-side conflicts in a constraint space.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.720
The speaking framework generates traceable implicit conflict samples.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:30.720
Applied to WildChat, it produced UC data, a 2,487-example training set.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:38.040
A Quinn 3.5-4B model trained on UC data outperforms larger general-purpose LLMs.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.080
Claude Opus 4.8 is among those outperformed.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.600
The evaluation uses the new UC bench conflict detection benchmark.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.840
The method explicitly models implicit incompatibilities between historical and current utterances.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.500
Reflective recovery extracts initial segments of failed trajectories.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.380
Those segments are concatenated with prompts.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.700
Models are trained to recognize and correct mistakes.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:14.000
On DeepSeek R1 to still Quinn 7B, the method raised accuracy from 30.0% to 37.5% on AIME

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:14.660
2025.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.220
Accuracy rose from 37.6% to 47.8% on Minerva.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.920
Emergent self-correction behaviors appear.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.480
The technique was evaluated across extensive benchmarks.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.360
It breaks the scaling collapse seen in pure imitation learning.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:36.460
Late fusion keeps each retrieval signal independent until the final ranking step.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.820
Early joint embedding is avoided.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.340
The three signals in trio rag each query the same multi-vector index.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.060
A weak image match can still surface if the VLM enhance text query scores strongly.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:53.540
This independence removes the need to maintain a single corpus level graph.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.760
The graph would otherwise need rebuilding whenever any document changes.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:04.580
On the reported benchmarks, the approach delivers 1.6 to 2.3 times faster per query inference.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.180
No cross-signal attention occurs until after the top-k candidates are already fetched.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.460
The trade-off appears when one mortality dominates.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.800
If the anchor image is the only reliable cue, the text only signals add noise.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.600
Late fusion must downweight that noise at ranking time.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.100
Teams facing rapidly changing document collections therefore prefer late fusion when update latency

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.800
matters more than the marginal gain from joint early fusion training.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:39.240
When the corpus is static and every modality is equally informative, early graph construction

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.340
can still justify its rebuild cost.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.700
The design choice trades a small amount of potential accuracy for substantially lower

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.880
maintenance overhead in production settings.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.620
Try Astra for Law if you handle US legal research.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:58.880
The legal search index surfaces authorities across 230 million URLs with daily updates.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:03.820
Run the Leia 421 million parameter model from the hugging face space.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:09.860
It tests calibrated decision scoring on intent routing or moderation tasks without a full

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:15.560
LLM forward pass. Experiment with TRIO RAG on your own multi-modal documents.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.740
See whether late fusion removes the need for a maintained corpus graph. Apply reflective recovery

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:27.180
to your own failed reasoning traces. Generate self-correction training data for smaller models.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.520
More partner and community plugins for legal workflows are expected as firms adapt the new

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.580
aster for law controls. Experimental results from the adaptive bio-protein design competition

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.080
will validate the 5,000-plus designs backed by anthropic credits.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:51.600
Additional RX-IV releases on user-side conflict detection and full duplex tool calling are anticipated in the coming weeks.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:58.300
Pop the hood on late fusion and you see why the independence of signals matters more than most retrieval papers admit.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.960
Each query runs separately against the same index, so a strong text signal can rescue a weak image match.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.020
No joint embedding forces every modality through the same representation bottleneck.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:15.120
That removes the constant rebuild cost of a corpus graph when documents change daily.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:21.660
The measured speed up of 1.6 to 2.3 times comes directly from skipping cross-signal attention

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.740
until after top-k candidates are fetched.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:28.980
The cost appears when one modality carries all the useful information.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:33.880
Text only signals then inject noise that must be down-weighted at ranking time.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.860
static corpore with balanced modalities can still justify early graph

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.580
construction. Rapidly changing collections favor late fusion because update

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:49.060
latency outweighs the marginal accuracy gain. Before we go, adaptive bio will

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:52.740
release the first experimental validation results from the protein design

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.800
competition next week. That's models and agents for today. If you found this useful,

